<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e8f0f3;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #333; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Talents Around Us</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Will Play the Drums in the Parade.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Exchange directions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present details of their performance to classmates (e.g., 'I will sing two songs in the Fiestas Patrias activities.' 'Liz is the Tambor Mayor. She enjoys leading the drummers in the parade, marking the rhythms, and keeping the group organized.').
            Can ask for directions related to the talent show location (e.g., 'Where is the stage?').
            Can provide basic information about their talent (e.g., 'I can play the drums in the parade.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e8f0f3;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different talents for Panama's National Holidays parade (e.g., a band playing drums/flute, a baton twirler, a dancer). Students quickly name the talent or instrument. The teacher asks, "What talent will you show in the parade?" to elicit initial ideas and review vocabulary from previous lessons. (e.g., "I will play the drums." "I will dance.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models dialogues for asking and giving directions to a "stage" or "performance area," incorporating target grammar.
                <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "Excuse me, where is the stage for the talent show?" (Points on a simple map/classroom drawing). "It's near the main entrance, next to the library. Go straight, then turn right. You will see it."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher models expressing plans: "I will play the accordion in the parade." "If I practice every day, I will perform beautifully!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher models asking about abilities: "Can you play the drums?" (Teacher plays an imaginary drum). "Yes, I can play the drums."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher highlights pronunciation of minimal pairs like /ʌ/ in 'fun' and /æ/ in 'band' by saying "It will be *fun* to play in the *band*." and having students repeat.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I will play the flute,' am I talking about today or the future?" (Expected answer: The future.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'Where is the stage?', what do I want to know?" (Expected answer: The location / how to get there.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Talents Around Us")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue between two students, Maria and Carlos, planning for a Fiestas Patrias talent event. The dialogue includes asking for directions to the 'stage' and sharing 'performance' details.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b><br>
                Carlos: "Hi Maria! Are you ready for the Fiestas Patrias event?"<br>
                Maria: "Yes, I'm so excited! I will dance the traditional 'tamborito'. What about you?"<br>
                Carlos: "I will play the drums in the band! But, excuse me, Maria, where is the stage for the dance performances? I need to know where to find you."<br>
                Maria: "Oh, it's easy! Go straight past the library, then turn left at the colorful flag display. The stage is right next to the big speaker. If you go too far, you will pass it."<br>
                Carlos: "Great! Thanks! Can you show me your dance steps later?"<br>
                Maria: "Of course! If I practice today, I will perform my best!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpersonal Communication (Exchanging information about plans, abilities, and directions).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the dialogue aloud while students follow along. Then, students answer:</p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> What are Maria and Carlos talking about? (Expected answer: Their talents and where to perform.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b>
                        <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Maria will sing. (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Carlos will play the drums. (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The stage is next to the library. (False - It's past the library, then turn left.)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using future simple for plans ('I will play the flute'), first conditional ('If I practice, I will perform beautifully'), and modal 'can' ('I can play the drums') to exchange directions and share talent details. Vocabulary: stage, parade, drums, accordion, flute, performer, practice, excited, talented, your, my, right, left, straight, next to.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Talent Show Plans" (Oral Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentence beginnings on the board or flashcards. Students complete them orally in pairs, focusing on future simple and 'can'.
                        <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Next week, I will _______ (play the drums / sing a song / dance)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My talent is ______ (playing the flute / dancing)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can _______ (twirl the baton / march in the parade)."</li>
                        </ul>
                        Teacher monitors for correct grammar and pronunciation (e.g., distinguishing 'drum' and 'hum' for rhyming words).
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice – "Direction Challenge" (Guided Dialogue):</b> Students work in pairs with a simple map of a school/park (showing 'stage,' 'library,' 'entrance,' 'flag display'). One student asks for directions to a specific place, and the other gives directions using sentence frames.
                        <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Excuse me, where is the _______ (stage / main entrance)?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Go _______ (straight / left / right). It's _______ (next to the library / near the flag)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Thank you! If I practice my dance here, will I perform well?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Yes, if you practice, you will perform beautifully!"</li>
                        </ul>
                        Teacher circulates, listening for correct use of directions and first conditional.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "Talent Interview" (Peer Check):</b> Students interview their partners about their talents and future plans for the parade or talent show. They use the modal 'can' and future simple.
                        <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What talent can you show, [partner's name]?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can _______ (play the flute / sing a song)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What will you do for the Fiestas Patrias activities?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I will _______ (march in the parade / be a baton twirler)."</li>
                        </ul>
                        Partners listen and politely check for clarity.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Talents Around Us")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Our Class Talent Show" Role-Play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. One student is a "New Student Performer" who needs to find their way around the school for the "Talent Show." The other student is a "Volunteer" who knows the school layout and the event details. They will role-play a conversation where the New Student asks about their performance time, talent, and directions to the 'stage,' 'changing room,' or 'waiting area.' The Volunteer provides this information and encouragement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher provides a simple floor plan of the school/event space with key locations labeled (Stage, Audience Area, Music Room, Library, Classroom 6A, Main Entrance, Flag Display).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive role cards with specific information:
                        <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>New Student Performer:</b> "My talent is [e.g., playing the flute]. I will perform in 10 minutes. Where is the stage? And where can I find my costume? I need to go to Classroom 6A first."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Volunteer:</b> Knows the map, knows the schedule, and can say things like: "Your stage is in the auditorium. Go straight from here, then turn right at the library. Classroom 6A is next to the music room. If you practice now, you will be amazing!" "I can help you find your costume. If you hurry, you will be ready on time!" They should use polite language (sociolinguistic competence).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pairs practice their role-play for 5-7 minutes. Teacher encourages students to be creative with their talents (e.g., 'I will play the accordion,' 'I will be a baton twirler,' 'I can sing a patriotic song.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Selected pairs perform their role-play for the class.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Our Class Talent Show" Role-Play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Our Class Talent Show" Role-Play, exchanging directions and sharing talent details.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can ask for directions (e.g., "Where is the stage?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can give basic directions (e.g., "Go straight, turn right").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use 'will' for future plans (e.g., "I will play the drums").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use 'can' for ability (e.g., "I can sing").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use first conditional for possibilities (e.g., "If you practice, you will perform well").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., stage, parade, talent, practice).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation is generally intelligible.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates respectfully with partner.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following sentences orally or on a small paper slip:</p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned how to say _______ (a new talent word / a direction word)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can now ask for directions to _______ (the stage / the school entrance)."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Consolidation and connection to future learning)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's speaking activities was most challenging for you? Why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How will you use what you learned today to talk about your own talents or plans for the Fiestas Patrias?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="margin-left: 15px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to promote speaking and interaction? (e.g., specific activities, use of visual aids, pair work structure).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., more practice with conditional sentences, clearer direction vocabulary, different grouping strategies).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson: This speaking lesson has prepared students to articulate their talent show plans and directions orally. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on this by having students write short paragraphs or instructions about their performance plans and location, using the same grammar and vocabulary.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>